{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And I will set your seed as the sand of the sea.</b> This promise was spoken to Abraham at the binding of Isaac, and there He mentioned the stars and the sand; and as it appears, the stars possess both multitude and preeminence, whereas the sand possesses only multitude alone. If so, why did Jacob not mention in his prayer the likening of them to the stars, which endure forever — and this would be closer to the matter for which he prays, that they endure and not be destroyed by the hand of Esau? And I say that since we find that Israel is likened to three things: to dust, to sand, and to stars — this is because in the time of tranquility and success they are likened to the stars, as it says: ״יָרוּם וְנִשָּׂא וְגָבַהּ מְאֹד״ (Isaiah 52:13), ״וּמַצְדִּיקֵי הָרַבִּים כַּכּוֹכָבִים לְעוֹלָם וָעֶד״ (Daniel 12:3), and thus Rashi explained the verse ״וְהִנְּכֶם הַיּוֹם כְּכוֹכְבֵי הַשָּׁמַיִם לָרֹב״ (Deuteronomy 1:10). And in the time of lowliness, when they are as dust to be trampled, it says ״וְהָיָה זַרְעֲךָ כַּעֲפַר הָאָרֶץ וּפָרַצְתָּ״ etc. (Genesis 28:14). And this is the promise that in Egypt they would be as dust upon which all plow, as it is written (Psalms 129:3) ״עַל גַּבִּי חָרְשׁוּ חֹרְשִׁים״ — at that time they would rise from the lowness of their station and spread westward and eastward, from one extreme to the other, as explained above in the portion וַיֵּצֵא (28:14). And in the intermediate time between the two, that is, in a time when they have no rest from the pursuing enemies who stand against them to destroy them and the Holy One, blessed be He, delivers them from their hand — they are likened to this sand, against which all the waves of the sea stand to flood the world, yet when they reach the sand they break and cannot pass beyond the sand. So, too, the nations that rise against Israel, as it is written (Psalms 42:8) ״כָּל מִשְׁבָּרֶיךָ וְגַלֶּיךָ עָלַי עָבָרוּ״ — they are unable to destroy Israel. And for this reason Israel is likened to sand. Therefore he mentioned in his prayer the matter of the promise of the sand, to be delivered from the hand of the pursuer Esau, and that he would not be able to prevail over him — just as these waves are not able to prevail against the sand, so Esau would not be able to prevail against him."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:39.858444769Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}